###############################
# Git Line Endings            #
###############################

# LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# A checkout on any platform, Windows included, gets LF, rather than each
# machine's `core.autocrlf` deciding, a per-machine setting nothing here
# records.
#
# `eol=lf` rather than bare `text=auto` normalises what is on disk as well as
# what is stored. Without it a Windows checkout can hold a shell script with a
# `bash\r` shebang, which fails with "bad interpreter".
#
# This marks nothing as modified. Git compares normalised content, so a CRLF
# working tree still matches an LF index; files convert as git next touches
# them.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The files whose interpreter is a Windows one keep CRLF on disk.
*.[cC][mM][dD] text eol=crlf
*.[bB][aA][tT] text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts are LF on disk. The rule above already says so; this line
# holds it for them if that rule changes.
*.sh text eol=lf

###############################
# Binaries                    #
###############################

# Declared binary so git neither diffs them nor changes their bytes. Without a
# rule git guesses per file from its contents.
#
# Not `filter=lfs`: binaries are ordinary git objects here, so a clone yields
# every file's bytes without `git-lfs`. With the filter declared, the first
# binary added would be stored as an LFS pointer, and a clone without
# `git-lfs` would hold pointer files in place of the contents.
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.psd binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.7z binary
*.br binary
*.gz binary
*.tar binary
*.zip binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.duckdb binary
*.db binary
